course: physics
lesson: 34
title: Wheels, axles, ramps and screws
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Try turning the thin metal bar inside a door when the handle has come off: it is really hard, yet with the handle on it turns with one finger; the handle is a wheel and axle, and a big wheel turns a small axle with a much stronger twist.
explain: A ramp is a slope that swaps a big lift over a short distance for a small push over a long distance, which is why a wheelchair goes up a ramp, not a step.
explain: A screw is a ramp wrapped round a rod: each turn creeps it in a little way with a big push, and jar lids and bottle caps are screws too.
explain: A wedge, like an axe head or a door stop, is a ramp that turns one downward blow into strong sideways pushes.
explain: None of these give you something for nothing: you push less, but you push further or turn more times.
fact: A wheel and axle turns a big wheel with a small force into a strong twist on the small axle; door handles, steering wheels, taps and fat screwdriver handles use it.
fact: Old lorries without power steering had huge steering wheels to give the driver more turning force.
fact: A ramp lets you raise something with a smaller push over a longer distance; a steeper ramp is shorter but harder to push up.
fact: Mountain roads zigzag so they are longer but much less steep, and cars and walkers can climb without slipping or straining.
fact: A screw is a ramp wrapped round a rod, and each turn moves it in a little way with a big push.
fact: A wedge, such as an axe head, a door stop or the edge of a knife, turns a push into sideways pushes that force things apart.
fact: On a playground roundabout, a push at the edge turns it more easily than a push near the middle, and riders at the edge move faster.
fact: A rolling wheel or ball has much less friction than a box sliding, which made the wheel one of the most important inventions in history.
fact: A rolling pin presses down on only a thin line of dough at a time.
fact: A tap's handle turns a screw thread that moves a valve up and down a little, which is why some taps need many turns to open fully.
exercise: Why is a door handle easier to turn than the thin bar inside it? | The big handle turns the small bar with a stronger twist | The handle is made of softer metal | The bar inside is glued to the door | The handle has a hidden spring inside that does the work for you
exercise: Why do screwdrivers have fat handles? | A fat handle turns the thin blade with more twist | Fat handles are cheaper to make | Fat handles keep your hand warm | A fat handle makes the screw go in longer and deeper
exercise: Why did old lorries have huge steering wheels? | A bigger wheel gives the driver more turning force | So the driver could see over them and spot the road ahead | Big wheels were the fashion back then | To make the lorry go faster
exercise: Why does a ramp help push a wheelchair into a bus? | A small push along a long slope is easier than lifting straight up | The ramp makes the wheelchair lighter | Ramps are slippery so wheels slide up | The bus pulls the wheelchair up the ramp
exercise: What changes if a ramp is made steeper? | It is shorter but harder to push up | It is longer and easier to push up | It is shorter and easier to push up | Nothing changes at all
exercise: Why don't roads go straight up mountains? | Zigzags make the road longer but much less steep | Straight roads are too short to drive on | Mountains are too hard to dig straight through | Zigzags help drivers stay awake
exercise: How is a screw like a ramp? | It is a ramp wrapped round a rod | It is a ramp folded flat into a disc | It is a ramp cut into lots of little pieces | It is nothing like a ramp, it is a kind of nail
exercise: Which of these is a screw? | A jar lid | A door handle | A rolling pin | An axe head
exercise: How does an axe split a log? | Its wedge-shaped head pushes the wood apart sideways | It burns a line through the wood | It sucks the wood apart | It pulls the log up from the ground
exercise: Which of these is a wedge? | A door stop | A steering wheel | A rolling pin | A jar lid
exercise: Where is a playground roundabout easiest to push? | At the edge | Near the middle | On the axle itself | Anywhere is just the same
exercise: Where do you go fastest on a roundabout? | On the edge | In the middle | Halfway in | Everywhere the same
exercise: Why does a ball roll more easily than a box slides? | Rolling gives much less friction than sliding | Balls are always lighter than boxes | Boxes are pulled down much harder by gravity than balls | Balls float slightly above the floor
exercise: Why was the wheel such an important invention? | It made moving heavy loads far easier, changing farming and travel | It was the first thing ever made of metal | It made people taller | It let people fly
exercise: Why is a rolling pin round? | It rolls along, pressing on a thin line of dough at a time | A round pin is lighter to lift and easier to wash up afterwards | Dough sticks only to round things | Round pins stay cold longer
exercise: Why do some taps need lots of turns to open fully? | A screw thread changes each turn into only a small up-and-down movement | The water is pushing hard against the handle | The tap has a lock that opens slowly | The pipes are very long
test: ugh the handle came off the door and now i can't open it with the stupid little bar. why's it so hard? | Without the big handle, you lose the wheel's extra twist | The bar is broken inside | Doors lock themselves tight the moment the handle comes off | The bar is much heavier than the handle
test: Sam says a steeper ramp is always better because it is shorter. What's the catch? | A steeper ramp needs a bigger push | A steeper ramp is always far too slippery | A shorter ramp breaks more easily under weight | There's no catch at all, Sam is quite right
test: A walker climbs a mountain by a zigzag path instead of straight up. What does she gain and lose? | Each step is easier, but she walks further | Each step is harder, but she walks less | She gains nothing and loses time | Each step is easier and the path is shorter
test: In a cartoon, a knight pushes his cart straight up a cliff. What would a real engineer build? | A long, gentle ramp or a zigzag road | A taller cliff | A heavier cart | Square wheels on the cart, so it grips the rock better
test: If wheels had never been invented, what would happen to moving heavy things? | It would be much harder, because sliding has far more friction | It would be just as easy | It would be easier, because boxes slide better | Nothing would need moving
test: Your mate pushes the roundabout from near the middle and it barely moves. What do you tell him? | Push at the edge, it turns more easily | Push harder near the middle, it just needs more muscle | Push the axle directly | Sit on it first
test: How could you hold two bits of wood together really tightly without glue? | Drive a screw through them | Push a rolling pin over them | Tie a wheel to them | Rest a door stop against them
test: Why is an axe thin at the edge and wide at the back? | It is a wedge that forces the wood apart | The wide back makes the whole axe lighter to swing | The thin edge holds more heat and burns the wood | It is just the old fashion for axes, nothing more
test: Kai's jar lid won't come off. Which simple machine is the lid? | A screw | A lever | A pulley | A wedge
test: shut up and turn the tap, it's taking ages. why so many turns tho | The tap is a screw that moves the valve only a little each turn | The water is stuck in the pipes | The tap is broken and slipping | Taps open faster if you shout
test: Why do the riders at the edge of a roundabout scream more? | They go round faster than the riders in the middle | They are heavier than the others | The edge of the roundabout spins the other way to the middle | The middle is warmer
exam: Identify the simple machine of which a screw is a modified form. | An inclined plane wrapped about a cylinder | A lever bent round so as to form a complete circle | A pulley from which the rope has been removed | A wheel which has been deprived of its axle
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which mountain roads are constructed in a zigzag pattern. | The gradient is reduced at the cost of a greater distance | The distance is reduced at the cost of a steeper gradient | The rock prevents the construction of straight roads | Drivers are thereby prevented from sleeping
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the advantage of rolling over sliding? | Rolling occasions considerably less friction | Rolling increases the weight of the load | Rolling eliminates the force of gravity upon the load entirely | Rolling requires a steeper surface
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the wheel and axle. | A force applied to the larger wheel produces a greater turning effect upon the axle | A force applied to the axle produces a greater turning effect upon the wheel | The wheel and axle create energy for the operator | The wheel and axle function solely upon inclined surfaces
